    "questionCode": "MH-9-MTH1-4-4.4.1-OSC-101",
    "text": "If \\(\\frac{a}{b} = \\frac{c}{d} = k\\), what is the value of \\(\\frac{2a + 3c}{2b + 3d}\\) in terms of \\(k\\)?",
    "options": [
      {
        "text": "\\(5k\\)",
        "isCorrect": false
      },
      {
        "text": "\\(k^2\\)",
        "isCorrect": false
      },
      {
        "text": "\\(\\frac{2}{3} k\\)",
        "isCorrect": false
      },
      {
        "text": "\\(k\\)",
        "isCorrect": true
      }
    ],
    "correctAnswer": "D",
    "solution": "Step 1: By the Theorem on Equal Ratios, if each ratio equals \\(k\\), then for any multipliers \\(l, m\\) (not both zero with non-zero denominator):\n\\(\\frac{2a + 3c}{2b + 3d} = \\frac{2(bk) + 3(dk)}{2b + 3d} = \\frac{k(2b + 3d)}{2b + 3d} = k\\).\nStep 2: Each resulting ratio remains equal to the original common ratio \\(k\\).\nStep 3: Hence, the value is \\(k\\).",
